    "question": {
      "text": "The directional derivative of $f(x,y)=x^3y^2+3xy$ at $(1,2)$ in the direction of unit vector which makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ with the $x$-axis is ________.",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$16\\sqrt{2}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$8\\sqrt{2}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{2}}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$\\frac{25}{\\sqrt{2}}$"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "D",
      "correctText": "$\\frac{25}{\\sqrt{2}}$",
      "explanation": "Given $f(x,y,z)=x^3y^2+3xy$. $\\nabla f=(3x^2y^2+3y)\\mathbf i+(2x^3y+3x)\\mathbf j$. At $(1,2)$, $(\\nabla f)(1,2)=18\\mathbf i+7\\mathbf j$. The unit vector making an angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ with the $x$-axis is $\\mathbf b=\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbf i+\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbf j=\\frac{\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt2}$. Therefore the directional derivative is $(18\\mathbf i+7\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\frac{\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{25}{\\sqrt2}$. Choice (D).",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
