    "question": {
      "text": "If $\\mathbf V=(2x-y)\\mathbf i-yz^2\\mathbf j-y^2z\\mathbf k$, then evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf V\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ where $C$ is the curve bounding the projection on the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=9$, $z>0$, in the $xy$ plane.",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$18\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$6\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$8\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$9\\pi$"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "D",
      "correctText": "$9\\pi$",
      "explanation": "By Stokes' theorem, $\\oint_C\\mathbf V\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\iint_S(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf V)\\cdot\\hat n\\,dA$. Here $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf V=\\mathbf k$. For $f=x^2+y^2+z^2-9$, $\\hat n=\\frac{x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k}{3}$, so $(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf V)\\cdot\\hat n=z/3$. The projection is the circular region $x^2+y^2\\le9$, and using $dA=\\frac{z}{3}\\,dx\\,dy$ gives $\\iint_R dx\\,dy=9\\pi$. Choice (D).",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
